Hey there! To write a method that requires an object to be invoked in Java (known as an **instance method**), you simply write a method *without* using the `static` keyword. 

Based on your notes (**OOP Java - Unit-I.pdf**, Page 21–22):

An instance method belongs to an object of the class rather than the class itself, which means you need to create an object of that class using the `new` keyword before you can call it.

Here is an example from your notes showing how to define and invoke an instance method:

```java
public class MethodExample {
    // 1. Defining an Instance Method (notice there is no 'static' keyword)
    void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello, Welcome to Java!");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating an object of the class
        MethodExample obj = new MethodExample();

        // Calling the instance method using the object reference
        obj.greet();
    }
}
```

