Hey! To read text files in Java using character streams, you want to use classes from the `java.io` package designed for 16-bit Unicode characters (like `FileReader`) rather than byte streams. 

Based on your notes (**OOP Java - Unit-III.pdf, Page 3**), character streams are specifically built to handle text-based data. Here's how you can use a `FileReader` to read a file character by character:

```java
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class CharacterStreamExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            FileReader fr = new FileReader("input.txt");
            int data;
            while ((data = fr.read()) != -1) { // Read character by character
                System.out.print((char) data);
            }
            fr.close();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Takeaways from your notes:
* **Why Character Streams?** While byte streams (like `FileInputStream`) handle 8-bit bytes (great for images, videos, or PDFs), character streams handle 16-bit Unicode characters, making them much better suited for text files, XML, and JSON (*OOP Java - Unit-III.pdf, Page 3 & 5*).
* **How it works:** `FileReader` reads two bytes at a time (characters) instead of single bytes, and it returns `-1` when it reaches the end of the file (*JP UNIT-III Files.pdf, Page 2*).
* **Other key classes:** If you need to handle text more efficiently or read line-by-line, you can also look into using `BufferedReader` along with your `FileReader` (*OOP Java - Unit-III.pdf, Page 3*). 

