Hey there! To create a thread using the `Runnable` interface, you follow a couple of simple steps instead of subclassing `Thread`. 

Based on your notes (`Java-Unit3 part2.docx` and `OOP Java - Unit-III.pdf, Page 11`), here is how you do it:

1. **Implement `Runnable`:** Create a new class that implements the `java.lang.Runnable` interface and override its `run()` method where you put your thread's task.
2. **Pass to `Thread` Constructor:** Create an instance of your implemented class, and then pass that reference as a parameter into the constructor of the `java.lang.Thread` class. (Since a `Runnable` object isn't a thread by itself, this step wraps it inside a `Thread` object).
3. **Call `start()`:** Finally, invoke the `start()` method on that `Thread` object to begin execution.

Here is a quick code example based on your materials (`JAVA UNIT-3 THREADS& UTIL PACKAGES.pdf, Page 5`):

```java
class MyThread implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread is Running Successfully");
    }
}

public class Program {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread g1 = new MyThread(); // 1. Create instance of Runnable class
        Thread t1 = new Thread(g1);  // 2. Pass it to the Thread constructor
        t1.start();                    // 3. Start the thread
    }
}
```

**Why use `Runnable` instead of extending `Thread`?** 
Since Java doesn't support multiple inheritance of classes, implementing `Runnable` leaves your class free to inherit from any other parent class you might need! Let me know if you want to look at examples for multi-threading as well.

Hey! To create a child class from a parent class in Java, we use the `extends` keyword. 

Based on your notes (`JP Unit II.pdf`, Page 1 and `JAVA-Notes-Unit-2_Part 1.pdf`, Page 2), here is the syntax:

```java
class Superclass-name 
{
    // parent class methods and fields
}

class Subclass-name extends Superclass-name 
{
    // child class methods and fields
}
```

The `extends` keyword essentially tells Java that you're building a new class that derives from an existing one, which helps increase functionality and promotes code reusability! Let me know if you want to look at an example with this.

Hey! Writing a class that implements an interface is pretty straightforward in Java. Based on your OOP Through Java notes (JAVA-Notes-Unit-2_Part 2.pdf, Pages 3–4), you use the `implements` keyword and provide the body for all the abstract methods defined in the interface.

Here is how you can set up a `Vehicle` interface and a `Car` class implementing it:

```java
// Define the Vehicle interface with abstract methods
interface Vehicle {
    void start();
    void stop();
    void accelerate();
    void honk();
}

// Class Car implementing the Vehicle interface
class Car implements Vehicle {
    private String brand;
    private String model;

    // Constructor to initialize brand and model
    public Car(String brand, String model) {
        this.brand = brand;
        this.model = model;
    }

    // Implementing the start method
    public void start() {
        System.out.println(brand + " " + model + " is starting.");
    }

    // Implementing the stop method
    public void stop() {
        System.out.println(brand + " " + model + " is stopping.");
    }

    // Implementing the accelerate method
    public void accelerate() {
        System.out.println(brand + " " + model + " is accelerating.");
    }

    // Implementing the honk method
    public void honk() {
        System.out.println(brand + " " + model + " is honking: Beep Beep!");
    }
}
```

A quick tip from class: when a class implements an interface, it *must* provide implementations for all of its abstract methods, otherwise Java will throw a compilation error unless the class is declared as `abstract`. 

