Hey! To show dots (or any other echo character) for a password in Java AWT, you can use a `TextField` and apply the `setEchoChar()` method. 

Based on your notes (`Java-Unit4-part1.docx`), `TextField` supports a password mode specifically through this feature. 

Here is how you can use it:

```java
import java.awt.*;

public class PasswordExample extends Frame {
    public PasswordExample() {
        setTitle("Password Field Example");
        setSize(300, 200);
        setLayout(null);

        // Create a TextField
        TextField passwordField = new TextField();
        passwordField.setBounds(50, 80, 200, 30);

        // Set the echo character to a dot ('*') or any other symbol
        passwordField.setEchoChar('*');

        add(passwordField);
        setVisible(true);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new PasswordExample();
    }
}
```

Whenever the user types in that `TextField`, it will mask the input characters with the symbol you passed into `setEchoChar()`! Let me know if you need help with anything else from Unit 4.
